  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p26-q11-introduction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Many of the remarkable properties of the Fibonacci numbers can be proved by induction. ",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p26-q11-definition",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Recall that the Fibonacci numbers are defined by ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "F_1=F_2=1",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", and the recurrence relation ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "F_{n+1}=F_n+F_{n-1}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for all ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "n\\geq2",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Prove each of the following:",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$F_1+F_2+F_3+\\cdots+F_n=F_{n+2}-1$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nEstablish $n=1$, then add $F_{k+1}$ to the assumed sum and use $F_{k+3}=F_{k+2}+F_{k+1}$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(n)$ be the stated identity.\n\nBase case: $F_1=1$ and $F_3-1=2-1=1$, so $P(1)$ is true.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $P(k)$ is true, so $F_1+F_2+\\cdots+F_k=F_{k+2}-1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}F_1+F_2+\\cdots+F_k+F_{k+1}&=(F_1+F_2+\\cdots+F_k)+F_{k+1}\\\\F_1+F_2+\\cdots+F_k+F_{k+1}&=(F_{k+2}-1)+F_{k+1}\\\\F_1+F_2+\\cdots+F_k+F_{k+1}&=F_{k+3}-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe second row uses $P(k)$ and the last row uses the recurrence. Thus $P(k+1)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, $P(n)$ is true for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$ by mathematical induction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$F_1+F_3+F_5+\\cdots+F_{2n-1}=F_{2n}$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nAdd $F_{2k+1}$ to the assumed sum and use $F_{2k}+F_{2k+1}=F_{2k+2}$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(n)$ be the stated identity.\n\nBase case: $F_1=F_2=1$, so $P(1)$ is true.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $P(k)$ is true, so $F_1+F_3+\\cdots+F_{2k-1}=F_{2k}$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}F_1+F_3+\\cdots+F_{2k-1}+F_{2k+1}&=(F_1+F_3+\\cdots+F_{2k-1})+F_{2k+1}\\\\F_1+F_3+\\cdots+F_{2k-1}+F_{2k+1}&=F_{2k}+F_{2k+1}\\\\F_1+F_3+\\cdots+F_{2k-1}+F_{2k+1}&=F_{2k+2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe second row uses $P(k)$ and the last row uses the recurrence. Since $2k+2=2(k+1)$, $P(k+1)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, $P(n)$ is true for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$ by mathematical induction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$F_2+F_4+F_6+\\cdots+F_{2n}=F_{2n+1}-1$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nAdd $F_{2k+2}$ to the assumed sum and use $F_{2k+1}+F_{2k+2}=F_{2k+3}$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(n)$ be the stated identity.\n\nBase case: $F_2=1$ and $F_3-1=2-1=1$, so $P(1)$ is true.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $P(k)$ is true, so $F_2+F_4+\\cdots+F_{2k}=F_{2k+1}-1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}F_2+F_4+\\cdots+F_{2k}+F_{2k+2}&=(F_2+F_4+\\cdots+F_{2k})+F_{2k+2}\\\\F_2+F_4+\\cdots+F_{2k}+F_{2k+2}&=(F_{2k+1}-1)+F_{2k+2}\\\\F_2+F_4+\\cdots+F_{2k}+F_{2k+2}&=F_{2k+3}-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe second row uses $P(k)$ and the last row uses the recurrence. Since $2k+3=2(k+1)+1$, $P(k+1)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, $P(n)$ is true for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$ by mathematical induction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$F_1^2+F_2^2+F_3^2+\\cdots+F_n^2=F_nF_{n+1}$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nThe inductive step gives $F_kF_{k+1}+F_{k+1}^2=F_{k+1}(F_k+F_{k+1})=F_{k+1}F_{k+2}$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(n)$ be the stated identity.\n\nBase case: $F_1^2=1$ and $F_1F_2=1$, so $P(1)$ is true.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $P(k)$ is true, so $F_1^2+F_2^2+\\cdots+F_k^2=F_kF_{k+1}$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}F_1^2+F_2^2+\\cdots+F_k^2+F_{k+1}^2&=(F_1^2+F_2^2+\\cdots+F_k^2)+F_{k+1}^2\\\\F_1^2+F_2^2+\\cdots+F_k^2+F_{k+1}^2&=F_kF_{k+1}+F_{k+1}^2\\\\F_1^2+F_2^2+\\cdots+F_k^2+F_{k+1}^2&=F_{k+1}(F_k+F_{k+1})\\\\F_1^2+F_2^2+\\cdots+F_k^2+F_{k+1}^2&=F_{k+1}F_{k+2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe second row uses $P(k)$ and the last row uses the recurrence. Thus $P(k+1)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, $P(n)$ is true for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$ by mathematical induction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Consecutive Fibonacci numbers $F_n$ and $F_{n+1}$ have no factor in common other than 1.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Consecutive Fibonacci numbers have no common factor greater than 1.\n\nA common factor of $F_{k+1}$ and $F_{k+2}$ also divides $F_{k+2}-F_{k+1}=F_k$, contradicting the inductive hypothesis.",
          "worked": "Let $P(n)$ be the statement that $F_n$ and $F_{n+1}$ have no common factor greater than 1.\n\nBase case: $F_1=F_2=1$, so $P(1)$ is true.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $P(k)$ is true, so $F_k$ and $F_{k+1}$ have no common factor greater than 1.\n\nInductive step: suppose an integer $d>1$ divides both $F_{k+1}$ and $F_{k+2}$. It also divides their difference. By the recurrence,\n$$F_{k+2}-F_{k+1}=F_k.$$\nThus $d$ divides both $F_k$ and $F_{k+1}$, contradicting $P(k)$. Therefore, $F_{k+1}$ and $F_{k+2}$ have no common factor greater than 1, so $P(k+1)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, $P(n)$ is true for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$ by mathematical induction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      }
    ]
  },
